WHAT COST MORE THAN AN HOUR

Refresh token rotation, and getting the persistence race right. Intuit rotates the refresh token roughly daily and kills the old value the instant a new one is created.
That means a crash between "got the new token" and "wrote it to disk" doesn't degrade well. It permanently disconnects the integration. TO fix this, I implemented atomic writes,
a cross-process lock so two server instances can't both refresh and invalidate each other, and re-reading credentials inside that lock so a losing process adopt's the winners token.

The MCP SDK's error model was the opposite of what I assumed it to be. I initially wrote every failure path to raise MCPError, thinking that was the proper typed error. It's actually
a protocol error. It never reaches the model at all. The whole tool call just fails silently from the model's POV. A plain Python exception is how I remedied the model's visibility into errors.
